>	- cockatrice

See the / about it. It's from Middle-Ages European mythos.

>	- lich

A very powerful form of undead, came to NH via D&D (and AD&D for Demilich etc).
I believe the concept comes from Germanic mythos, with lich having the same
root as moderns Swedish lik = corpse.

>	- xan

A mythical insect from the Popol Vuh, the Central American Indian creation
myth. I don't remember for sure, but I think it was the Mayas' myth. If it
was someone else (Aztecs?) I apologise to any who might be offended by  my
ignorance.

>	- mattock (yes, I've read the discussion, it's just an example)
>	- Mjollnir, Excalibur etc.

Mjollnir was the war hammer of Thor, which turned into a thunderbolt when
he cast it. It was too heavy to be wielded by anyone except Thor himself,
and one other god, Magni, who was the personification of strength. Norse mythos.

Excalibur was King Arthur's sword, which he received from the Lady of
the Lake. It was cast back into the lake when Arthur died, and the same
hand accepted it.

Stormbringer is from Michael Moorcock's Elric of Melnibone series of
fantasy novels. The NH item is a watered-down version (necessarily!).
The books are fun, read them.

Orcrist and Sting are from J.R.R. Tolkien's The Hobbit (and the Lord
of the Rings). Orcrist is Quenya, and means "goblin-cleaver".

The crysknife is from Frank Herbert's Dune novels. A knife made from 
the tooth of a sandworm.

The palantir of Westernesse is from JRR Tolkien's books. Means "far-seeing".

